  "fibres": [],
  "measures": [],
  "notes": "Dichroic atomic vapour laser lock (Corwin et al. 1998) on the rubidium D2 line. A linearly polarised probe crosses a natural rubidium cell in an 80 G field along it (a solenoid or ring magnets in the lab). The field shifts the σ+ lines up and the σ− lines down, so the two circular halves of the linear light are absorbed differently. A quarter-wave plate at 45° turns the two hands into p and s, and a polarising cube sends them to two photodiodes. Their difference, A − B, crosses zero at the centre of the Doppler-broadened line with a steep slope over a range as wide as the line itself: an error signal a laser can be locked to without modulation. Select the cell to see the σ+ (blue) and σ− (orange) spectra; press Play on the laser’s sweep and watch the two diodes swap. Raise the field to widen the capture range.",
  "ui": {
